### Lab 8: reservation events through RabbitMQ.
### Producer: reservation-service (via the web app on 8080). Consumer: notification-service (8083).
### RabbitMQ management UI: http://localhost:15672 (queue notification-service.reservation-events).

### 1. Business action: create a reservation (201). The producer publishes RESERVATION CREATED after commit.
POST {{host}}/api/reservations
Authorization: Bearer {{employeeToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "resourceId": "{{resourceId}}",
  "startTime": "{{startTime}}",
  "endTime": "{{endTime}}"
}

### 2. Result of the consumer: one stored notification per event (200). No HTTP call between the two services.
GET {{notificationHost}}/api/notifications
Authorization: Bearer {{employeeToken}}

### 3. Business action: cancel the reservation (copy its id to reservationId). Publishes CANCELLED.
POST {{host}}/api/reservations/{{reservationId}}/cancel
Authorization: Bearer {{employeeToken}}

### 4. Negative: overlapping reservation fails (409) and publishes no event.
POST {{host}}/api/reservations
Authorization: Bearer {{employeeToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "resourceId": "{{resourceId}}",
  "startTime": "{{startTime}}",
  "endTime": "{{endTime}}"
}

### 5. Negative: no token (401)
GET {{notificationHost}}/api/notifications

### Scenario 4 (consumer stopped): docker compose stop notification-service, run steps 1 and 3 (both succeed),
### the queue keeps 2 messages, then docker compose start notification-service and repeat step 2.
### The third event, COMPLETED, is produced by the Quartz expired-reservation cleanup after a reservation ends.
